<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="indole-3-acetate biosynthesis I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWYDQC-4" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Ubiquitous Plant Pathways (UPP) that was generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most land plants. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; The phytohormone family of auxins regulates many plant biological processes including cell division, elongation, differentiation, root initiation, tropic responses, flowering, fruit ripening, and senescence |CITS:[15749753]|. |FRAME: INDOLE_ACETATE_AUXIN &quot;Indole-3-acetic acid&quot;| (IAA) is the most abundant natural plant auxin. This is the main tryptophan-dependent pathway for auxin biosynthesis in |FRAME: TAX-3702| and most likely other plants as well |CITS: [22025724][22155950]|. Prior to the work of |CITS: [22025724]|, each of the 2 reactions in this pathway were believed to participate in separate tryptophan-dependent pathways for auxin synthesis, one involving |FRAME: INDOLE_PYRUVATE| (IPA) and one involving a |FRAME: TRYPTAMINE| intermediate (see |FRAME: PWY-581| for a diagram that integrates the various pathways that have been suggested for IAA biosynthesis). However, it has now been shown that both enzymes participate in a simple pathway that generates |FRAME: INDOLE_ACETATE_AUXIN| from |FRAME:TRP| in two steps |CITS: [22025724]|. Studies of |FRAME:TAX-3701| mutants indicate that |FRAME: INDOLE_ACETALDEHYDE|, previously considered an intermediate in the conversion of IPA to IAA, is not formed |CITS: [22025724]|. The pathway has been confirmed in several ways, including expression of the |FRAME:TAX-3701| |FRAME: AT4G13260| protein in &lt;i&gt;E coli&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [22025724]|. Mutations in the orthologous maize genes |FRAME: GDQC-114715| and |FRAME: GDQC-111218| greatly reduce auxin levels and have severe phenotypes, indicating that this pathway is important in maize as well |CITS: [18799737][21335375]|. Double |FRAME: GDQC-114715|/|FRAME: GDQC-111218| mutants have the same reduction in auxin levels as the |FRAME: GDQC-111218| single mutant, supporting the conclusion that these genes function in the same pathway |CITS: [21335375]|.</Description>
